  "worked_solution": "First, we find $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}+9y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-6y+9) & =0 \\\\ y(y-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082348/assets/7509bf6a10838458b110d982740c1399aa36279b9ce4a231f99b76d7c7cee0b3.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x={y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}+9y$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=3.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}({y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}+9y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-6{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+9{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3}-6\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{3}+9\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{4}}{4}-0)-6(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-0)+9(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}-54+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
